```{r setup}
#| include: false
library(tidyverse)        # data wrangling and plotting
library(moderndive)       # get_regression_() functions
library(ggfortify)        # autoplot() in ggplot layout
library(gt)               # publication-quality tables
library(broom)            # tidy model output
library(patchwork)        # combine ggplots
library(car)              # VIF for multicollinearity diagnostics
library(sandwich)         # Robust covariance matrix estimators
library(lmtest)           # coeftest() for hypothesis tests with robust SE

# shared visual identity
theme_set(theme_minimal(base_size = 11))

# colour palette
col_primary   <- "#D50006"   # main highlight (Store 114)
col_secondary <- "#502479"   # contrast (Store 83)
col_neutral   <- "#686868"   # grey reference

# load data — both files come from data/raw/
date_stamp <- "20260517"  # adjust to match the most recent DataImport.R run
tbl_stores <- read_rds(xfun::from_root("data", "raw",
                       glue::glue("dominicks_stores_{date_stamp}.rds")))
tbl_demo   <- read_rds(xfun::from_root("data", "raw",
                       glue::glue("dominicks_demo_{date_stamp}.rds")))

# convenience subsets — filtered on-the-fly from the full panel
tbl_s114 <- tbl_stores %>% filter(store == 114)
tbl_s83  <- tbl_stores %>% filter(store == 83)
```

# Scenario — A Translator Briefing at Dominick's Finer Foods

You work as a data analyst for **Dominick's Finer Foods**, a Chicago
supermarket chain operating 83 stores. This morning, an email from
Sarah Chen, VP Marketing, has reached your team.

:::{.callout-note icon=false collapse="false"}
## 📧 Memo from Sarah Chen, VP Marketing — Dominick's Finer Foods

*"Team, two decisions are pending and I need your help.*

***1. Q4 advertising campaign for the Dominick's house brand.***
*Marketing wants to invest in a feature campaign. Before signing off,
the executive board wants to know: **what is the effect of advertising
on sales?** We have years of weekly data. Surely we can answer this
from the historical record?*

***2. Inventory planning tool for all 83 stores.***
*Operations is building a forecasting tool to set weekly Orange Juice (OJ) stock
across our 83 stores. Can you give them **a prediction model** that
works across the whole chain?*

*How would you approach these?"*

---

— Sarah
:::

The two questions sound similar at first — but they require fundamentally different analytical approaches. 

This lab is your first deep dive into the two major paths of quantitative analysis:
- **Path E (Explanation)** — understanding *how* and *why* things work (i.e. how and why advertising affects sales)
- **Path P (Prediction)** — building reliable forecasts for new, unseen situations

As a **Translator** between business and analytics, your job is to recognize which path a question actually needs and to clearly communicate what can realistically be delivered — and what cannot.


