---

**Notes on the bonus tasks**

- **B1** is the **direct continuation** of the diagnostic work in Step 1a. Do this one if you want to deepen your understanding of what the diagnostics tell you (and what they don't).
- **B2** is the most **operationally useful**: heteroscedasticity-robust SE are the default in most modern applied econometrics.
- **B3** reconnects to the **resampling toolkit** from Session 2 and prepares you for situations where classical inference becomes shaky.
- **B4** is the **bridge to Lab 2** and to all subsequent panel-data work. It is also the most consequential in practice — most analysts who handle panel data without clustering produce overconfident results.
- **B5** demonstrates how to convert a diagnostic flag into a **quantitative robustness statement** — the kind of practice that distinguishes credible from glib applied work.
<!-- - **B6** serves as a **direct preview** of the machine learning approach we will explore next. It gives you a head start on the regularisation techniques covered in Lab 2. -->

If you complete all five, you have a complete picture of the inference-side toolbox: diagnostics → robust SE → bootstrap → clustering → sensitivity. This allows you to rigorously verify whether your `mod_baseline` suffers from structural misspecification or unobserved heterogeneity (e.g., OVB) before moving to the prediction-side toolbox (regularisation, cross-validation) in Lab 2.

---

# What comes next


| Lab | Question it answers |
|-----|---------------------|
| **Lab 2 — Regularisation on demographics** | Can we improve the prediction tool by adding demographic variables — and which of them matter? |
| **Lab 3 — A/B-test design**                | How would we set up a study that answers Sarah's Question 1 properly? |


**What Lab 2 will add to this story.** Step 3 already showed that 
out-of-sample performance is worse than the in-sample fit suggests. 
Lab 2 takes this further: we will see that *OLS itself* — by 
insisting on unbiased coefficients — is suboptimal for prediction. 
Methods that deliberately introduce bias (Ridge, Lasso) can lower 
the total prediction error. We will also see what happens when we 
push the OLS model into an overparameterised regime: in-sample 
$R^2$ approaches 1, while out-of-sample performance collapses — a 
concrete demonstration of the bias-variance trade-off announced in 
the BLUE bridge.

**A first picture of the causal structure (preview of Lab 3).** 
The mini-DAG from Step 1b — `deal` as common cause of `feat` and 
`logmove` — is the entry point into causal graph theory. Lab 3 
generalises this with directed acyclic graphs (DAGs), the rules for 
identifying causal effects from observational data, and the methods 
of causal inference (A/B testing, DiD, IV).

